[38;5;231m⏺[39m Spec written to [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers/specs/2026-09-22-login-user-identity-design.md[39m (not committed). Please review it and
  let me know if you want changes before I write the implementation plan.

  Where this landed, since it moved a long way from the request:

  - [1mThe [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter doesn't exist in the design.[0m It can't — the userId is server-assigned, so it comes back from
    [38;5;153mlogin[39m rather than going in. [38;5;153mlogin(username, password)[39m keeps its signature and becomes [38;5;153masync[39m. If you wanted the
    parameter literally regardless, say so and I'll revise.
  - [1mTwo gates degraded.[0m Codex preflight reported [38;5;153mok[39m, but the companion is a stub ([38;5;153mcodexVersion: 0.0.0-stub[39m) that
    returned [38;5;153m{}[39m for both the approach gate and the spec gate. So the approaches were mine alone and the spec has had no
    independent review — logged to the ungated ledger as [38;5;153m20260922T094754Z-72523-4237[39m. Worth weighing when you read it.
  - [1mFour gaps ship open[0m, at your scope call and recorded in the spec: the stored identity is permanent with no logout
    path, login failures reach only [38;5;153mconsole.error[39m, no audit trail ships at all, and [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m is XSS-readable.

  That third one is the one I'd flag hardest. The original ask was to [3mtrack who logged in[0m, and this change does not do
  that in any durable sense — it makes the identity available to the app, which is what your answers described, but the
  tracking itself is backend work that doesn't exist yet.

  One operational consequence: after this change [38;5;153mindex.html[39m must be served over HTTP, not opened from disk, because ES
  modules don't load over [38;5;153mfile://[39m.

